"Real backend, real authentication and real seed data for local development and testing of the Waldorf community task manager (Hungarian UI), on branch feature/real-backend-local. Today the app only runs in synthetic mode: every domain area uses in-memory fakes with a fixed set of fictitious data, login is a four-profile picker plus a parent-only /belepes that accepts only parent-local, and the production runtime mode is a 20-line stub with no Supabase client (src/runtime/production/composition.ts); only two SQL migrations exist in supabase/migrations (access rls and current roles) and nothing runs them. The owner needs a local environment with real data and a real workflow to test the mechanism end to end: (1) real authentication and sign-in with real sessions, so that several parents can be signed in as different people at the same time and their interaction can be tested (task sign-up, swaps and cancellations between families, notifications per parent, invitations through /meghivas/<token>); (2) real group admins and super admins with real scoped permissions as defined by the existing access model and migrations; (3) real records in a real database: groups, families, children, school years, events, tasks, assignments, approvals, notifications, reminders; (4) a repeatable local seed with several parents, families, groups, a group admin, a super admin and a teacher, plus reset; (5) the app switches between synthetic and real mode by configuration (APP_RUNTIME_MODE) without changing the screens, replacing the in-memory fakes behind the existing ports with real database adapters, keeping the synthetic mode working for the automated tests. Decide in the plan how much runs locally (for example Supabase in Docker versus a hosted project), which data layers come first, how real authentication works with invitations, and what stays out of scope (real email delivery, deployment). Team constraints: Hungarian user-facing copy, English identifiers, trunk-based short-lived branches, green checks before merge, 80% coverage, accessibility, no deployment workflows until a deployable app and a linked target project exist, never commit secrets or real personal data, real-backend checks must be exercised against a real target before being called verified. The owner asked first for the work to be scoped as an intent: produce clear requirements and design with phases, and stop for the owner decisions at the gates."
